    "story": "Reaching for the rattle\n\nLearning Story\n\nMere was lying on her tummy on the mat with a wooden rattle just out of reach. She reached towards it, rocked her body forward, and kept working out how to get close enough. On her third go, Mere reached far enough to grab the rattle.\n\nOnce it was in her hand, Mere shook the rattle and looked at us. She babbled, “ba ba”, then rolled onto her back and kept shaking it. Mere was using her body, her voice, and the rattle all together in this small but focused moment of play.\n\nWhat learning we noticed\n\nMere showed persistence as she tried more than once to reach the rattle. She was beginning to use her body with purpose, rocking forward and adjusting her movement until she could get what she wanted. When she looked at us and babbled, she may have been sharing her success or inviting a response.\n\nMere also explored cause and effect. She moved the rattle and heard or felt the result, then kept shaking it after rolling onto her back. This showed us that she was interested in what the rattle could do and how she could make it happen again.\n\nCurriculum links\n\nThis links with Mana aotūroa | Exploration, especially children using movement and active exploration to make sense of the world. Mere reached, rocked, grasped, rolled, and tested the rattle through her body.\n\nThis also links with Mana reo | Communication, as Mere used babbling, looking, and sound-making with the rattle to communicate and connect.\n\nWhere to next / Responding\n\nWe can continue to place safe objects just within Mere’s reach so she can practise reaching, shifting her weight, and making choices. We can sit nearby, respond to her sounds, and give her time to try before offering help.\n\nWhānau link\n\nMere may enjoy similar tummy time play at home with a safe rattle or small toy placed where she can see it and work towards it.",
