    "story": "Leading the waiata actions\n\nLearning Story\nAt mat time, Nikau stood at the front with Whaea Sue for Tūtira mai ngā iwi. He joined in by singing the first line loudly, then became quieter while he kept doing the actions. At the end of the waiata, Nikau clapped.\n\nWhat learning we noticed\nNikau showed confidence by standing at the front with Whaea Sue. Singing the first line loudly showed he knew part of the waiata and was willing to use his voice in front of the group. When he went quiet, he still stayed with the waiata through the actions. This tells us Nikau could keep taking part in a way that worked for him in that moment.\n\nClapping at the end showed Nikau recognised the waiata had finished and joined in with the group rhythm and routine. He was using both voice and movement to be part of mat time.\n\nCurriculum links\nThis links with Mana reo | Communication, as Nikau used singing, movement, and actions to express himself during a familiar waiata.\n\nThis also links with Mana whenua | Belonging, as Nikau joined a shared mat time routine with Whaea Sue and the group. Tūtira mai ngā iwi gave him a familiar way to connect through waiata and actions.\n\nWhere to next / Responding\nWe can keep offering Nikau chances to join waiata in different ways, such as singing, doing actions, clapping, or standing near a kaiako. We can notice whether he chooses to lead again, sings more of the waiata, or uses the actions to stay involved when he is not using his voice.\n\nWhānau link\nWhānau might like to share whether Nikau sings or does actions to favourite waiata at home.",
