    "story": "The hut door password\n\nLearning Story\nTui, Rawiri and Sam built a hut using crates and a sheet. In the play, Tui took on the job of looking after the door. When someone came to the hut, Tui asked, \"password please\".\n\nSam answered with \"banana\", and Tui let him in. This small part of the play showed Tui adding a clear rule to the hut game and helping the group keep the pretend idea going.\n\nWhat learning we noticed\nTui was using pretend play to organise the space and the roles within it. By being in charge of the door, Tui gave herself a clear part to play and used language to explain what needed to happen next.\n\nWe noticed Tui using social thinking too. She made a rule that others could follow, listened for Sam’s answer, and responded by letting him in. The password idea helped the group share a simple story together.\n\nCurriculum links\nThis links with Mana reo | Communication, as Tui used words to set up and continue the pretend play. The phrase \"password please\" gave the game a clear structure and invited others to respond.\n\nThis also links with Mana tangata | Contribution, as Tui took responsibility for a role in the group play and helped others join in through the shared rule.\n\nThere is also a link with Mana aotūroa | Exploration, as Tui and the other children used crates and a sheet to create a new play space and build a working idea together.\n\nWhere to next / Responding\nWe can support this interest by offering time and space for hut building, with loose parts that children can move and arrange safely. We can listen for the rules and roles the children create, then support them to negotiate these with each other.\n\nWe will continue to notice how Tui uses language in pretend play, especially when she sets up roles, invites others in, or changes the rules of the game.\n\nWhānau link\nAt home, whānau might notice Tui creating rules or roles in pretend games, such as door keepers, shops, houses, or secret places.",
