    "story": "Writing her name and noticing words\n\nLearning Story\nSophie wrote her name on her drawing today. She formed the S backwards, and the rest of the letters in her name were in the right order. This showed us that Sophie is thinking carefully about the letters that belong to her name and is beginning to notice how each letter is formed.\n\nAfter this, Sophie looked at the lunch box sign and copied the word KAI. Once she had written it, she asked what it spells. Sophie was not only copying the letters she could see, she was also trying to understand that print carries meaning.\n\nWhat learning we noticed\nSophie is building her confidence as a writer. Writing her own name shows a strong connection to identity, and copying KAI from a sign shows she is noticing print in the environment and using it for her own writing. Her question tells us she wanted to make sense of the word, not just copy the shapes.\n\nCurriculum links\nEYLF Outcome 5: Children are effective communicators. Sophie used drawing, name writing, copied print, and a question to connect marks and letters with meaning.\n\nEYLF Outcome 4: Children are confident and involved learners. Sophie noticed print around her, copied it, and followed up with a question to extend her own understanding.\n\nWhere to next / Responding\nWe can support Sophie by naming the letters and sounds in her own name during natural writing moments. We can also draw her attention to familiar words around the room, especially words connected to daily routines, and talk about what they mean.\n\nWe will continue to offer real reasons to write, such as naming drawings, making signs, labelling work, or copying words that matter to her. We can gently support the direction of the S through playful practice, without taking over her writing.\n\nFamily link\nAt home, Sophie might enjoy noticing names, labels, signs, or lunch box words and talking about what they say.",
