    "title": "Sorting and making ten",
    "story": "Sorting and making ten\n\nLearning Story\nJack sorted the bear counters by colour into the cups. After sorting, he lined up the red bears and counted them. Jack counted 8 red bears, then added 2 blue bears to the end of the line and said, “now it's ten”.\n\nWhat learning we noticed\nJack used sorting, counting and adding in one clear sequence. He noticed the colours, organised the bears into groups, counted the red bears, and then changed the set by adding two more. When Jack said, “now it's ten”, he showed that he was thinking about the total, not just naming the colours or counting by rote.\n\nThis was a useful number moment. Jack was working with one-to-one counting as he lined up the bears, and he was beginning to combine quantities to make a new amount. Adding 2 blue bears onto 8 red bears showed early addition thinking in a hands-on way.\n\nCurriculum links\nEYLF Outcome 4: Children are confident and involved learners. Jack sorted, counted, organised and solved a number problem using the bear counters.\n\nEYLF Outcome 5: Children are effective communicators. Jack used words to share his mathematical thinking when he explained that the total had become ten.\n\nWhere to next / Responding\nWe can continue to offer small counting materials so Jack can sort, line up, count and combine groups in different ways. We can support his thinking with simple prompts such as, “How many are there now?” or “What could we add to make ten?”\n\nWe will continue to notice how Jack counts sets, whether he checks the total after adding more, and what number strategies he uses when the groups are changed.\n\nFamily link\nAt home, Jack might like simple number games using everyday items, such as counting fruit pieces, blocks, cars or pegs, then adding a few more to see the new total.",
